A||N||6||SpeedPhreak||Heeeeeeelllpp! clutch problems||||8
Z||000000||SpeedPhreak||11-16-1999||02:11 PM||||ok, you'll have to excuse my lack of mechanical vocabulary; but i need some help.  i have an 84 se 4 speed manual.<P>on my way to work i was at a stop light.  i had a problem shifting in to 1st & then the car died.  it wouldnt start & the clutch seemed stuck on the floor.  i pushed it to the side of the street.  what happened was there is a plastic piece that a rod from the slave cylinder pushes on. this plastic piece is conected to a metal arm that operates the clutch.  the plastic piece broke.  i went to get a new one & the dealership said it was discontinued & the whole arm was replaced with a new metal arm that has a indentation for the metal rod where the plastic piece used to be.  so i got it & installed it.  i then bled the system for air.  <P>ok so now i can start the car.  but it still wont shift.  if i have the car in gear it immediatly starts to move when the engine is started.  if the clutch was not a hydrolic clutch i would say that the freeplay needs adjusted.  but everyone tells me that hydrolic clutches are self adjusting.  there was no sign of clutch wear before it went out & it moves really well when you start it in gear.  there is only 1 way for the new arm to go on so i know its on right.  the only thing i noticed that i cant figure out is that the rod from the slave cylinder doesnt start to move until the clutch is alread 1/2 way down.  i had my dad (who used to be a mechanic) & another person look at it & they both say everything looks right but cant figure it out.  i explained it to the pontiac service guys & they say "bring it in" well i dont have $40 to have them tell me whats wrong with it.  so can u help me?<P>thanks <BR>Speed Phreak<P>ps after just reading a few replies to previous questions...could it be my throught bearing?  i cant go out i check right now cause im stuck in bed do to femur bone surgery but will as soon as im walking again.||||reg||8
Z||000001||Patrick||11-16-1999||03:59 PM||||<BR>Off the top of my head, I'd say you have air in the hydraulic system.<P>If you do a search for "clutch", you'll find all kinds of threads dealing with this issue. For the mother of all clutch threads, have a look at:<P> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/000034.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/000034.html</A> <P>Reading that'll keep you out of trouble for awhile. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000002||Carrolles||11-16-1999||04:32 PM||||I agree with the air in the system analysis.  The last time you pushed in the clutch and the plastic thing broke the slave cylinder pushrod probably overextended.<P>The bleed tap on the slave is on the feed end of the body which means that when you open the bleeder and push down on the clutch pedal you are only expelling the air from the front end of the body.  You need to compress the pushrod into the body while the bleeder is open to get the air that is trapped at the back end of the body out.  It is often easiest to do this by taking the slave loose from the transmission and doing it by hand.<P>On my 85SE 2m6 4spd I also get the left side of the car up on blocks or a curb during bleeding to help the air make its way to the high point.<P>Using a manual vacuum pump to bleed the system is less messy than just squirting it out of the bleeder.  I picked one up at Walmart on their clearance aisle for $15.00 and it has come in handy bleeding clutches and brakes.<BR>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||11-16-1999||08:24 PM||||I haven't had the pleasure of bleeding a clutch on a Fiero yet, but I've heard other members say that the V8 Archie method of doing it works. Check it out!<P> <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/archism.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/archism.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Patrick||11-16-1999||09:20 PM||||<BR><B>Pleasure ??!!!</B> ||||reg||10
Z||000005||Thomas||11-18-1999||10:16 AM||||I have bleed my clutch oftly in the last 7 jears.<BR>The most important I learn was:<BR>The best way to bleed the clutch is under pressure.<BR>I meen, use a brake bleeding system.<BR>The system press the fluid under pressure over the master cylinder into the clutch system.<BR>Only at this way you can clean the clutch from air realy.||||reg||10
Z||000006||DJRice||11-18-1999||12:44 PM||||While your at it, since the car is an '84, you probably should go hunt down a steel clutch pedal, because the one you have now is aluminum and will bend out of shape.<P>I used the v8 archie method mentioned above on my '84 and it got me back in business.<P> ||||reg||1
